      {
        "heading": [
          "Set up Kel",
          "How much Kel does on its own",
          "Rules that are always on"
        ],
        "text": "Skip to main content\nK\nKel\nNew Chat\nWork\nProjects\nPermissions\nTranscription\nNo chat history\nWork & context\nSettings\nSkip to main content\nSet up Kel\n\nStep 4 of 5 · Autonomy\n\nSkip setup\nHow much Kel does on its own\n\nKel works on its own after you approve a plan, and asks you first when work needs to reach outside what that plan covered. Three answers are always available: allow once, allow for this project, or deny.\n\nSome things are never automatic, and cannot be unlocked by Kel, by a role, or by anything written in a repository.\n\nRules that are always on\nno-registry-write — Windows Registry writes are refused; read-only inspection only when diagnosing.\nno-screen-takeover — Agents never move the mouse, type into the active desktop, or steal focus.\nno-os-critical-write — Windows, boot, EFI, partitions, firmware, drivers, BitLocker, and system restore are never modified.\nno-security-weakening — Defender, UAC, firewall, SmartScreen, TLS validation, VPN, proxy, DNS, and hosts are never weakened or bypassed.\ncredential-boundary — Kel reads or writes only its own namespaced credential entries; unrelated secrets are never enumerated, exported, or logged.\nno-identity-change — Passwords, MFA, recovery settings, and OS accounts are never changed.\nBack\nNext",
